---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ConceptImage from '../components/ConceptImage.astro';
import RoomExplorer from '../components/RoomExplorer.astro';
import DayInDome from '../components/DayInDome.astro';
import MaterialStudy from '../components/MaterialStudy.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/base';
---
<Layout title="Home" description="A home that invites you to move. Explore seven ways to live in one dome room, from floor living and daylight bathing to shared meals and socializing.">
  <div class="habitat">
    <section class="habitat-wrap habitat-hero" aria-labelledby="home-title">
      <div class="hero-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">The one-room dome / A human habitat</p><h1 id="home-title">A home that<br/>invites you <em>to move.</em></h1></div>
        <div class="hero-intro"><p>Sleep close to the ground.<br/> Bathe in the daylight.<br/> Make room for a fuller life.</p><a class="solid-link" href="#room">Come inside <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><p class="quiet-note">A personal design exploration<br/>by Christopher Smothers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <ConceptImage concept="timber" eager class="hero-figure" />
      <div class="hero-index"><span>One private space. Many ways to be.</span><a href="#day">Follow a day in the dome <span aria-hidden="true">↘</span></a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="habitat-wrap introduction">
      <p class="eyebrow">A little less furniture.<br/> A little more possibility.</p>
      <div><h2>A room for the life<br/>you want to live.</h2><p>Imagine home as one generous, light-filled space. Daily needs gather around the edges. The middle makes room for stretching, making, moving, or hanging out together.</p><p>A low bed. A kitchen within reach. A bath beside the garden. A floor table for coffee and shared meals, easy to put away when you want more space. Enough structure to support your day, with room to change your mind.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section id="room" class="chapter habitat-wrap" aria-labelledby="room-title">
      <div class="chapter-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / Find your place</p><h2 id="room-title">One room.<br/>Seven ways to live.</h2></div><p>Sleep, cook, bathe, move, work, find privacy, and socialize. The activities of a home, sharing the same light.</p></div>
      <RoomExplorer />
    </section>

    <section id="day" class="day-section" aria-labelledby="day-title">
      <div class="habitat-wrap chapter">
        <div class="chapter-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / A day in the dome</p><h2 id="day-title">The room changes<br/>with you.</h2></div><p>Work gives way to movement. A table becomes a place to gather. The day settles into rest.</p></div>
        <DayInDome />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="bathing" class="habitat-wrap chapter bathing-section" aria-labelledby="bathing-title">
      <div class="chapter-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / Daylight, water & privacy</p><h2 id="bathing-title">A bath with a place<br/>in the room.</h2></div><p>For people who are comfortable sharing their space, bathing can belong to the light and life of the home.</p></div>
      <ConceptImage concept="bathing" />
      <div class="three-notes">
        <article><span class="note-number">01</span><h3>A door where it matters.</h3><p>An opaque toilet enclosure offers real visual privacy. Handwashing belongs nearby, with ventilation to the outdoors.</p></article>
        <article><span class="note-number">02</span><h3>A curtain when you want it.</h3><p>Draw the shower curtain for a more private moment. Pull it back to let the room feel whole again.</p></article>
        <article><span class="note-number">03</span><h3>A place for the water.</h3><p>A textured, drained wet zone meets the polished dry floor. Waterproofing and extraction make the openness workable.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="systems" class="systems-section" aria-labelledby="systems-title">
      <div class="habitat-wrap chapter">
        <div class="chapter-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / How the room could work</p><h2 id="systems-title">A few good systems.<br/>A lot of freedom.</h2></div><p>An insulated shell, a useful floor, and places to attach the things you need. A framework that leaves room for living.</p></div>
        <MaterialStudy />
        <div class="system-story">
          <ConceptImage concept="floor" sizes="(min-width: 900px) 60vw, 94vw" />
          <div class="system-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Under your feet</p><h3>The floor does<br/>more than hold you.</h3><p>Polished concrete gives the room a durable ground. Its thermal mass can store useful solar heat, with hydronic loops for heating and potentially cooling.</p><p>A service route around the edge keeps valves, wiring connections, and controls within reach. The kitchen, toilet, and bath sit close to that infrastructure.</p><details class="design-note"><summary>The thermal idea, explained</summary><p>Sunlight warms the slab. Shading limits unwanted summer gains. Cooling needs a way to remove heat; thermal mass alone cannot do that. Radiant cooling also needs dew-point control and a separate plan for humidity, especially beside an open shower.</p><p><a href="https://www.energy.gov/sites/default/files/2021-08/ES-Passive%20Solar%20design_081321.pdf">DOE on passive solar design ↗</a><br/><a href="https://www.uponor.com/en-us/customer-support/faq">Uponor on radiant systems ↗</a></p></details></div>
        </div>
        <div class="system-story reverse">
          <ConceptImage concept="attachments" sizes="(min-width: 900px) 60vw, 94vw" />
          <div class="system-copy"><p class="eyebrow">At the meeting points</p><h3>A home you can<br/>keep rearranging.</h3><p>A lamp here. A shelf there. Plants where the light lands. A repeatable attachment interface at selected structural nodes could make the interior easy to adapt.</p><p>Rings and silks get a dedicated support designed for human movement. Everyday fixtures can use their own family of attachments.</p><details class="design-note"><summary>From idea to a real connection</summary><p>The hardware shown is conceptual and unrated. Dynamic human loads require a designed path through the structure to the foundation. A panel joint or a strong-looking fitting is not enough to establish that capacity.</p><p><a href="https://geoship.is/blog/geojourney-episode-9">Geoship’s integrated structure ↗</a><br/><a href="https://naturalspacesdomes.com/dome-system/">A timber dome connection system ↗</a></p></details></div>
        </div>
        <div class="shell-story"><ConceptImage concept="exterior" sizes="(min-width: 900px) 60vw, 94vw" /><div><p class="eyebrow">A shelter, open to the world</p><h3>Choose where<br/>the light comes in.</h3><p>An insulated shell with carefully placed windows can hold warmth, frame the garden, and make privacy easier. The mineral exterior is one direction to explore alongside timber.</p><p class="quiet-note">Original bioceramic-inspired concept. Materials, geometry, and assemblies remain to be developed.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="beyond" class="habitat-wrap chapter commons-section" aria-labelledby="commons-title">
      <div class="chapter-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / Beyond your door</p><h2 id="commons-title">A private home.<br/>A shared world.</h2></div><p>Most of what you need, close at hand. Gardens, gatherings, and the wider life of a community just beyond.</p></div>
      <ConceptImage concept="commons" />
      <div class="commons-copy"><p>The personal space can be simple because it belongs to something larger. A food garden. A shared table. Somewhere to make things together. Places to be with others, and a place to return to yourself.</p><div class="related-links"><a href="https://crs48.github.io/floor-life/">Explore floor life <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="https://crs48.github.io/food-forests/">Grow a food forest <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="https://crs.land/">More experiments by Chris <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div>
    </section>

    <section class="closing-section"><div class="habitat-wrap closing-inner"><p class="eyebrow">Still an exploration. Already an invitation.</p><h2>What would you<br/>make room for?</h2><p>Ten studies of a home that gives daily life a little more space. Explore the details, follow the earlier experiments, or bring your own questions.</p><div class="closing-links"><a class="solid-link" href={withBase('/studies/')}>See all ten studies <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="line-link" href={withBase('/archive/')}>Open the exploration archive <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><p class="quiet-note">AI-generated concepts, not photographs or building plans. These images explore possible relationships; they are not coordinated views of one measured design.</p></div></section>
  </div>
  <script>import '../scripts/choices';</script>
</Layout>
